export const DE_C1_S02_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-nachtprogramm": [
    `The programme was called "Nachtfalter" ("Moth"), ran from midnight to four in the morning on a small Saarbrücken station, and Nina Becker had been presenting it for seven years. Anyone listening to the radio at that hour was a lorry driver, a nurse, a baker or sleepless, and most of those who rang in belonged to the last group. Nina had learned to listen to them without comforting them, because comfort, she knew, quickly sounded like advertising at three in the morning. Her voice was deep and calm, and some listeners wrote to her that they could only fall asleep when she was talking. She took it as a compliment, albeit one with a barb in it.`,
    `The caller the team called "the bird man" rang every Thursday at around half past two. He called himself Gerd from Völklingen, had worked at the steelworks for forty years and talked about birds. About the blackbird that sang in his garden every morning before all the others. About the starlings that circled in huge flocks over the disused blast furnaces in autumn, as if looking for something that was no longer there. He never told her anything personal. Nina liked him. He was the only caller who never wanted anything from her.`,
    `Her own father had left the family when she was nine. One morning he had gone to his shift and not come back, and afterwards her mother had never spoken his name again, as if you could undo a person retrospectively that way. All Nina knew was that he lived somewhere in Saarland. She had never looked for him. What for? Whoever wanted to leave should leave. That was her principle, and it had proved its worth in seven years of night-time radio.`,
    `One night in November the bird man sounded different. His voice was hoarse and he spoke more slowly than usual. He didn't talk about birds but about the stars. He said that long ago he had taught a little girl who was afraid of the dark the constellations, so that she would have something to do at night. "She liked Cassiopeia best," he said. "Because it looks like a W. And the little one said the W stood for wach, awake, because you had to be awake to see it."`,
    `Nina heard her own voice say: "That's a lovely memory, Gerd." It sounded calm and deep as always. Her hand, on the other hand, had pulled the fader of her microphone down a little without her noticing. Cassiopeia. The W. She hadn't thought of that word for thirty years, and now there it lay, in the middle of her programme, like a stone someone had thrown through the window. She looked at the display. The number was withheld, as always.`,
    `"What became of the girl?" she asked. Her producer Tobias looked up behind the glass, because personal questions weren't her style. At the other end there was a long silence. "I don't know exactly," the bird man said at last. "I think she works at night. I think she has a very beautiful voice." Then he said, "Thank you for letting me call," as he did every Thursday, and hung up.`,
    `Nina played a song, then another, then the news. She presented the rest of the programme so flawlessly that Tobias told her afterwards she had rarely been so good. She nodded. At four o'clock she didn't go home. She asked him to find the number for her in the system, which stored every call, even the withheld ones, which wasn't actually allowed. Tobias, who in seven years had never heard her ask for anything, did it without asking a single question.`,
    `She stepped out into the car park behind the broadcasting house. The night was clear and cold, and above the roofs, where it had always been, stood Cassiopeia. Nina dialled. It rang four times. Then someone picked up and said nothing, and she heard him breathing, the way she had not wanted to hear anyone breathe for thirty years. "The W," she said. A pause. Then, very quietly: "Stands for awake."`,
  ],
  "de-das-ungeheuer-vom-weissensee": [
    `In Unterlaggen on the Weissensee, a municipality with 340 inhabitants, two inns and a mayor who had been re-elected for fourteen years because nobody else could be found, something had happened this year that Hannes Moser considered a catastrophe: nothing had happened. The number of overnight stays had slumped by a third. Tourists went to the Wörthersee, where there were yachts, or to Hallstatt, where there was Instagram. The Weissensee had only turquoise water, a great deal of peace and quiet, and a sewage plant the village was extremely proud of.`,
    `The idea came to Moser one evening at the Seewirt inn, over his third quarter-litre of wine, and when he said it out loud nobody laughed at first, which he took as a good sign. A monster. Like in Scotland. Only smaller, cosier, more Carinthian. The municipal secretary, Frau Wutte, a practical woman who carved nativity figures in her spare time, said after a moment's thought that she could deliver a head by Easter. Made of Swiss stone pine. With glass eyes.`,
    `On Easter Monday, shortly after sunrise, Moser rowed out into the fog in a fishing boat, towing the head behind him on a long line, just below the surface of the water. On the shore, as if by chance, stood Frau Wutte's nephew with his phone. The video lasted eleven seconds and was blurred, shaky and completely convincing. By the evening it had two million views. By Wednesday the monster had a name, "die Weissi", by Friday its own hashtag, and by the weekend both inns were booked out until September.`,
    `It turned into a summer such as Unterlaggen had never known. The bakery sold Weissi doughnuts, the tobacconist Weissi postcards, and the Seewirt offered a "Monster Snack Platter" that differed from the ordinary snack platter only in its price. Moser gave interviews in a traditional costume he had bought specially, and spoke with a serious face of "unexplained phenomena". He had never in his life, he confessed to his wife, enjoyed lying so much.`,
    `In August a man arrived from Graz. His name was Dr. Pirker, he was a limnologist, that is, a lake researcher, and he brought a boat full of measuring equipment, including a sonar with which, as he kindly explained, you could detect any fish over twenty centimetres. Moser slept badly for three nights. Frau Wutte hid the head in her garage under a pile of Christmas tree stands. As a precaution, the nephew deleted all the videos from his phone.`,
    `After a week Dr. Pirker presented his report. In the lake he had found char, pike, a sunken tractor from the fifties and remarkable water quality. He had not found a monster. "But that doesn't mean there isn't one," he said at the press conference, and Moser almost hugged him. "It only means that I haven't found one. In some places the lake is almost a hundred metres deep. There's room for a lot down there." After that, the inns were booked out until Christmas.`,
    `One morning at the end of October, when the tourists had gone and the first frost lay on the meadows, Moser rowed out once more on his own. He didn't want to film anything. He just wanted to see what the lake looked like without an audience. The fog lay thick on the water, it was completely still, and Moser was just thinking that next year he perhaps ought to tell the truth after all when the boat rose. Only a little, only a few centimetres, as if something very large had very slowly breathed out underneath it. Then a shadow glided beneath the boat, longer than the boat itself, and vanished into the depths.`,
    `Moser rowed back faster than he had rowed since his youth. He called Frau Wutte before he had even got out of the boat. "The head," he gasped. "Where's the head?" "In my garage," said Frau Wutte. "Under the Christmas tree stands. Where else would it be?" Moser sat down on the jetty. He decided not to tell the truth next year after all. The thing was, he was no longer entirely sure which one it was.`,
  ],
  "de-die-wand-von-hassel": [
    `There are matches people will still be talking about in a hundred years, and there are matches in the Kreisliga B in Gelsenkirchen. The game between SC Glückauf Hassel and DJK Eintracht Buer last Sunday belongs, as your reporter may reveal in advance, to the first category, even though only 143 spectators saw it, including two dogs and a man who was actually just looking for his key.`,
    `The situation was one of classical tragedy. On the last matchday Glückauf needed one point to avoid relegation, Buer needed three for promotion, and it was raining the way it only rains in the Ruhr in May: horizontally and with personal intent. After twelve minutes Glückauf's goalkeeper Kevin Brzezinski injured himself trying to take a back pass on his chest, in a place that shall not be described here in more detail. The club had no reserve goalkeeper. It hadn't had one at all since 2019.`,
    `Coach Ralf Szymanski did what coaches in the Kreisliga do at such moments: he looked around. His gaze fell on the kiosk at the edge of the pitch, where Heinz Grabowski has been selling beer, newspapers and mixed bags of sweets since 1991. Grabowski is fifty-eight years old, has a belly that is rightly proud of itself, and last played in goal in 1989, in a season known at the club only as "the season of the Wall", because he supposedly didn't let in a single penalty. Whether that's true, nobody can check today. Grabowski himself has never denied it.`,
    `"Heinz," shouted Szymanski, "you have to!" Grabowski looked into the rain, then at his till, then into the rain again. "What do I get for it?" he asked. "Eternal glory," said the coach. "Got that already," said Grabowski. Then he took off his apron, locked up the kiosk, borrowed Brzezinski's gloves, which were too small for him, and a spectator's woolly hat, which was too big for him, and stepped onto the pitch to a cheer such as the ground on Kanalstraße had not heard in decades.`,
    `What happened next can only be adequately described with the means of epic poetry. Grabowski saved. He saved with his belly, with his knee and once with the back of his head, and one close-range shot he saved simply by standing there, so wide and so unshakeable that the striker evidently decided he would rather shoot past him than hit him. Glückauf took the lead through an own goal by the visitors, Buer equalised after the break, and when the clock on the clubhouse showed the eighty-ninth minute, it was 1-1. One point. Enough.`,
    `Then, in the ninetieth minute, a Buer player went down in the penalty area and the referee pointed to the spot. Whether it was a foul will still be debated in Hassel long after this newspaper has ceased to exist. Stepping up to take the penalty was Dennis Kaya, nineteen years old, Buer's top scorer, who grew up three streets away from Kanalstraße. Grabowski looked at him for a long time and, as witnesses report, with a certain tenderness. Then he positioned himself not in the middle of the goal but half a metre to the left.`,
    `Kaya shot to the left. Grabowski hardly had to move. The ball bounced off his belly and out for a throw-in, the referee blew the final whistle, and 143 spectators, two dogs and the man with the key stormed the pitch. Glückauf were safe. Asked how he had known where Kaya would shoot, Grabowski told your reporter, while already unlocking the kiosk again: "For twelve years that lad made up every mixed bag of sweets himself at my place. Always starts on the left. First the little cola bottles, then the sour snakes. What a person does as a kid, he does in the penalty box too."`,
    `For the sake of completeness it should be reported that the kiosk was completely sold out that evening. At around eight, when the rain finally eased off, Dennis Kaya came by too, in his Buer tracksuit, with wet hair. He bought a one-euro mixed bag. Your reporter, who happened to be standing nearby, can testify that for the first time in his life he made it up starting from the right.`,
  ],
  "de-rezepte-eines-toten": [
    `Johanna Albers had taken over the Löwen-Apotheke in Halle's old town in the spring, along with a drawer full of mysteries. Her predecessor Werner Pohle had retired at seventy-eight, after more than fifty years behind the same oak counter, and at the handover had given her a bunch of keys, a tomcat called Hufeland and a sentence to take with her that she didn't understand at the time: "Frau Riedel gets whatever's written on it."`,
    `Frau Riedel came on a Tuesday in May. She was small and very upright, wore a hat despite the warmth, and laid a prescription on the counter the way you play a card you know will win the trick. Johanna read it. Issued by Dr. med. Kurt Riedel, specialist in internal medicine. Prescribed: peppermint tea, one packet. Valerian tablets, one packet. Below that, in the same slanting handwriting: "A quarter of an hour of conversation." Johanna looked up. "Your husband?" "My husband," said Frau Riedel. "He's been dead for nineteen years. But he made very thorough provisions."`,
    `Johanna knew what she had to do. A prescription from a deceased doctor was invalid, she had learned that in her first semester, and a pharmacist who dispensed invalid prescriptions risked her licence. On the other hand, Frau Riedel wasn't asking for anything that needed a prescription at all. Tea. Valerian. A conversation. Johanna gave her the tea and the valerian, took no money because Frau Riedel didn't offer any, and talked to her for a quarter of an hour about the weather, the river Saale and the cat. When the old lady had gone, she rang Pohle.`,
    `"Ah, Frau Riedel," said Pohle, and his voice softened. "So she still comes. Good." He wouldn't say any more on the phone. He only said that in the bottom drawer on the left there was an envelope she should read if she thought it necessary. Johanna thought it necessary. She found the envelope under old price lists, yellowed, addressed to "Werner" and dated February 2005.`,
    `"Dear Werner," Dr. Riedel had written, "when you read this I will be dead, and Elfriede will have found my prescription pads. She finds everything. She won't fall ill, she's tougher than the two of us put together. She'll just be alone, and she won't tell anyone, because she's too proud. But she will hand in a prescription, because a prescription isn't a request, it's an order. Give her whatever's written on it. Send me the bill, wherever I am. Yours, Kurt."`,
    `Johanna read the letter twice. Then she understood the drawer. In it lay, neatly bundled, hundreds of prescriptions from nineteen years, all in the same handwriting, which, as she now noticed, had grown shakier and shakier over the years, as if the doctor had been getting older in his grave too. "Two pieces of Bienenstich cake." "A walk to the Peißnitz island, accompanied." "One hour of reading aloud, Fontane, not the poems." Next to each one, in Pohle's tiny writing, was a tick.`,
    `From then on Frau Riedel came every Tuesday. Johanna found that she looked forward to it, and that she studied the prescriptions, whose handwriting soon became so familiar to her, carefully, not for their validity but for their handwriting. She never mentioned what she knew. Frau Riedel never mentioned that she suspected it. And so the summer and the autumn passed.`,
    `In December Frau Riedel laid a prescription on the counter that had no medicine on it. The writing was so shaky that it took Johanna a long time to decipher it. "Company, daily. Until further notice." Johanna looked at the old lady, and the old lady held her gaze without blinking, like someone playing her last card. Then Johanna turned the sign on the door to "Lunch break", although it was only half past ten, and put the water on for the peppermint tea.`,
  ],
  "de-spiegelgasse-1916": [
    `Zurich, 6 February 1916. Since yesterday we have had artists in the back room. The landlord has let it to a German, a tall, pale gentleman with a wife who sings, and on top of that young people from every country under the sun come every evening: Romanians, Alsatians, a painter who looks like a priest. They call the whole thing a cabaret and have painted the name of a French philosopher on the sign. The landlord says that as long as they pay for the beer they can call it whatever they like. I serve.`,
    `12 February. I don't understand what the gentlemen are doing in there, and I don't think they understand it either, and that is apparently the point. Yesterday three of them recited poems at the same time, each a different one, in three languages, while another banged on a frying pan. The audience whistled, and the gentlemen bowed as if whistling were the finest praise. Out there at the front people are dying by the thousands, says the apprentice who reads the newspaper. In here people are dying of laughter, or of annoyance, depending.`,
    `3 March. Today for the first time the gentleman from number fourteen came downstairs. He and his wife are lodgers at the shoemaker Kammerer's, a small man with a bald head and a reddish beard, very polite, very firm. Could the music not be made a little quieter, he had to work. I said I would pass it on. He thanked me as if I had signed a contract for him and went back upstairs. The gentlemen in the back room laughed and drummed even louder. I passed it on all the same. Nobody is going to say that a waitress doesn't keep her word.`,
    `23 June. Tonight the tall German appeared in a costume made of cardboard, shiny blue, with a hat like a chimney, and sang words that don't exist. Gadji beri bimba, something like that. At the end he had to be carried off the stage because he couldn't move in his cardboard. He looked like a bishop who had fallen into a tin can. I laughed, and that embarrasses me, because I think I laughed in the right place. I don't want to start understanding these people.`,
    `2 July. The gentleman from number fourteen often sits in the library now, says Frau Kammerer, from morning till night, writing about imperialism. Today I met him on the stairs as I was carrying the empty bottles down. He asked how much I earned and how long I worked. I told him. He nodded as if he had guessed, and said that one day people like me would no longer serve people like those in the back room. I asked who would serve then. He had no answer to that, or he considered the question beneath him.`,
    `End of July. The cabaret is closed. The gentlemen have fallen out, or the money has run out, or both; with them you never quite know. The back room still smells of tobacco and glue. The landlord is glad. So am I, I think. On the last evening the Romanian wrote me a poem on a napkin that consists entirely of numbers. I've kept it, I don't know why.`,
    `9 April 1917. Today the gentleman from number fourteen left. By train, they say, right across Germany, in the middle of the war, in a carriage nobody is allowed to enter. Frau Kammerer is crying a little because she was fond of him, even though he always paid the rent to the exact rappen and not a rappen more. In the morning he came in to us once more, drank a coffee standing up, put twenty rappen on the table as a tip and gave me a piece of advice to go with it: I should organise myself. Then he left, very quickly, like someone who has to catch a train that will change the world.`,
    `10 April. I have thought for a long time. The gentlemen in the back room wanted to change the world with nonsense, the gentleman from number fourteen with seriousness. Which of them will do more harm, I don't know. The gentlemen in the back room, at any rate, paid for their beer, mostly.`,
  ],
};

